  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-07T08:26:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "237e59be-c4bb-4c7e-9ad8-dfd47886f4a2",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 070826\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n126 AM MST Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Well above normal temperatures will persist through the end of the \n  work week as lower desert highs top out in the triple digits.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for portions of southeast\n  California and southwest Arizona today through Friday as \n  temperatures warm to near record levels, resulting in areas of \n  major HeatRisk.\n\n- Dry conditions will persist through at least the end of the\n  workweek before a much more unsettled pattern arrives by the\n  weekend with much cooler temperatures and increasing rain\n  chances. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest objective analysis shows a broad ridge of high pressure \nover the Desert Southwest with a very weak disturbance near the AZ\nand UT border. This disturbance combined with enough mid-level \nmoisture may help spark a few showers mainly over the White \nMountains this afternoon and Thursday afternoon as it moves \nsoutheastward through the Four Corners. Otherwise, with high \npressure in control, dry conditions will prevail through much of \nthe area through the rest of the week. \n\nThe main story through the end of the week will be the well-above \nnormal temperatures. 500 mb heights today and Thursday will range \nbetween 586-590 dam with 850 mb temperatures peaking between 25-27C. \nThis will translate to surface high temperatures surpassing the \ntriple digits across much of the lower desert communities with \nreadings ranging between 98-103 degrees across south-central AZ\nand 104-108 degrees across the western deserts. With temperatures\n10-13 degrees above normal and nearing daily records in Yuma and \nEl Centro, areas of Major HeatRisk are expected and thus Extreme \nHeat Warnings are in effect starting today for the Imperial Valley\nand Yuma with the warning expanded to include the Lower Colorado \nRiver Valley and along I-8 in Yuma County for Thursday when the \nhottest temperatures are expected. By Friday, even though the \nridge will weaken as a broad troughing pattern begins to impinge \ninto the west coast, low-level thermal profiles will still remain \nwarm enough for afternoon highs to once again surpass the triple \ndigits, with readings ranging between 98-101 degrees across south-\ncentral AZ and 104-107 degrees across the western deserts. With \nareas of Major HeatRisk continuing in Yuma and the Imperial \nValley, the Extreme Heat Warnings will remain in effect for these \nareas.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nA significant pattern shift is still anticipated this weekend \nthrough early next week as the most of the deterministic and \nensemble model guidance continues to show a broad troughing \npattern setting up across much of the western third of the CONUS. \nGuidance continues to show a northern stream trough diving into \nthe Pacific Northwest on Saturday before sinking southward over CA\non Sunday. Meanwhile, a more southern stream shortwave trough is \nlikely to move into the subtropical eastern Pacific and pick up \nthe remnants of Hurricane Rachel as it moves northeastward and \nphases with the west coast trough. As this whole pattern evolution\ntakes shape, the increasing south to southwesterly flow aloft \nwill advect moisture into the Desert Southwest starting Saturday, \nwith most of the moisture at first confined in the mid to upper-\nlevels before better low-level moisture moves in on Sunday as the \nremnants of Rachel gets pulled in. Coincidentally, rain chances \nwill be on the increase as early as Saturday afternoon across \nsouth-central AZ before peaking Sunday into Monday across much of \nthe region with the arrival of better low-level moisture. There \ncontinues to be a great deal of uncertainty in rainfall amounts as\nit will be dependent on the overall track of the remnants of \nRachel and how it interacts with the west coast trough. It will \nlikely take at least another couple of days to get a better idea \non the rainfall perspectives as guidance comes into better \nagreement on how the overall pattern shapes up. In addition to the\nrainfall potential, winds will be another hazard to contend with \nas the combination of the height falls from the trough and remnant\ncirculation of Rachel will likely result in widespread breeziness.\n\nAs 500 mb heights lower with the approaching trough along with \nthe increasing cloud cover and moisture, much cooler temperatures\nare expected this weekend into early next week. Afternoon highs \nacross the lower deserts will go from mid to upper 90s on Saturday\nto low to mid 80s by Monday and may potentially drop even lower \non Tuesday with many locations stuck in the low 80s. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0530Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo weather concerns will exist through Wednesday night under a few \nmidlevel cloud decks. Confidence is very good that the trends in \nlight winds and timing of directional shifts will be nearly \nidentical to the past 24 hours. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather concerns will exist through Wednesday night under clear \nskies. Extended periods of light and variable winds will be common \nwith a tendency towards for northerly component backing to a west \ntrajectory after sunset. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions along well-above normal temperatures will continue\nthrough the end of the work week as lower desert highs reach into\nthe triple digits. Winds through Thursday will generally be light\nand diurnally driven before increasing Friday into this weekend. \nAfternoon MinRHs through the end of the work week will range \nbetween 15-25% with fair to good overnight recoveries (35-60%). A \nmore unsettled weather pattern is expected this weekend through \nearly next week as a weather system affects the area with much \ncooler temperatures along with increasing moisture and chances for\nwetting rainfall. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\n\nRecord highs:\n \nDate Yuma      El Centro\n----     ----     ---------\nOct 7    111 in 2024105 in 1991\nOct 8 107 in 1996    106 in 1996\nOct 9 106 in 2024104 in 1996\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM MST Friday \n     for AZZ532.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM to 8 PM MST Thursday for AZZ530-\n     536.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM PDT Friday \n     for CAZ566-567.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM to 8 PM PDT Thursday for CAZ569-\n     570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Lojero\nAVIATION...18 \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero \nCLIMATE...RW\n",
